In article <1991Apr2.020944.9293@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG> xanthian@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG (Kent Paul Dolan) writes:
>kevin@rotag.uucp (Kevin Darcy) writes:
>
>>Well, if it really is -choking- (i.e. a piece of food getting stuck in the
>>windpipe/trachea (?) as a result of careless eating habits or 
>>under-mastication), then I would expect it to, if anything, be less likely
>>to occur if the player is slowly and methodically ingesting the food, and
>>-more- likely when the player is famished, and is just tossing it down
>>willy-nilly. I.e. less likely to occur when the player is full/slow - the
>>reverse of the current game behavior.
>
>This is an incorrect perception;...

Kent, I said "if anything", meaning that I had serious doubts whether there
would be -any- variation in ingestion rates, but, IF THERE WAS, it would
probably be in the opposite direction than the current choking code &
rationalization would suggest.

>...More hasty still will be the adventurer trying to shovel down the
>immense corpse that is known to provide an exceptionally valuable
>intrinsic, after an awsome battle that probably awoke every monster on
>the level. 

No, I would expect a famished person to wolf his/her food down more hastily
than someone trying to acquire an arcane intrinsic. Hunger is a very powerful,
very -primal- biological drive...

>Of _course_ choking is a possibility, and choking is nearly
>_guaranteed_ when one is trying to bolt food into a stomach whose gorge
>is rising in response due to already being satiated from recent similar
>behavior. 

And, as the gorge rises, the ingestion rate drops... At least, this is my
experience.

>The game is already _very_ realistic in how it treats eating.

I disagree.

>> To the user, slowing the rate of consumption at high satiation levels
>> is -already- a fact of dungeon life.
>
>No, that's _not_ what's happening; you have a binary chokes/survives
>choice,... 

Incorrect. A code fragment from eat.c: 

	    if (u.uhunger >= 1500) {
	      if(!victual.eating || (victual.eating && !victual.fullwarn)) {
		pline("You're having a hard time getting all of it down.");
		nomovemsg = "You're finally finished.";
		if(!victual.eating)
		    multi = -2;
		else {
		    victual.fullwarn = TRUE;
		    if (victual.canchoke) {
#ifdef MACOS
			if(!flags.silent) SysBeep(20);
			if(UseMacAlertText(128, "Stop eating ?") == 1)
#else
			pline("Stop eating? ");
			if(yn() == 'y')
#endif
			{
				reset_eat();
				nomovemsg = NULL;
			}
		    }
		}
	      }
	    }
	}

Note the setting of multi to -2. If you will peruse allmain.c, you will find
that this prevents the player from performing any useful activities until
an additional 2 moves pass. This wasted 2 moves in effect -slows- the 
player's ingestion rate (i.e. it increases the amount of time required to 
consume a given quantity of food).

>...not an "eat no faster than you digest current stomach contents"
>choice. That latter is totally unrealistic, and models no human behavior
>observable anywhere.

Do you deny that consumption rates drop as the subject becomes more satiated?
Do you have hard medical evidence that forcing down food when one is full 
increases the chance of choking on a given food particle? You claim that what
I suggest is not "observable", but neither is the current NetHack choking 
behavior "observable" anywhere I'm aware of. The question on the table is,
"has the current NetHack eating model been optimally -extrapolated- from 
observed behavior?"

>> Furthermore, the "Stop eating?" prompt would disappear, along with the
>> false sense of security it exudes (this, by the way, would be a return
>> to earlier versions of NetHack which had no such prompt).
>
>Which (keep eating while monsters hit you multiple times and you die) was
>set up for totally bogus behavior, the massive complaints against which
>got the current "stop eating(?)" prompt installed.

Kent, I've never suggested allowing monsters to pound on the player several
times while he or she is enjoying a repast. I -have- suggested that the 
player continue eating until attacked, and immediately cease. There are 
already facilities in the code to support such an implementation (think about 
how pick-axe digging or tin-opening works, for instance).

>Look, nowhere on the planet do you see people who sit and eat while
>ignoring enemies who are beating on them.  Arguably, someone somewhere
>every day chokes to death from trying to eat too much or too fast; the
>Heimlich maneuver became famous and widespread so quickly because the
>problem it solved was so pervasive and well known, not just because it
>was a cute way to hug someone.

I can see how eating too -fast- can raise the chance of choking. I can see how
eating too -much- can put one's life at risk due to the chance of stomach
rupture, or the deliterious effects to one's -whole- system because of 
"digestive overload". I do -not- see how eating when full can increase choking 
chances, because there does exist an undeniable slowing effect in this case.
The ONLY question of realism which remains in my mind is whether the slowing 
phenomenon is psychosomatic or not.

>Leave it ALONE!

Conclusions:

	a) I do not disagree that real dungeon-eating would probably almost 
	   always be a hasty affair. My point is that, IF ANYTHING, it would 
	   be even -more- hasty when the player is hungry, not the other way 
	   around.

	b) The current code does already support a crude form of "slowing".

	c) While it is impossible to perfectly model eating behavior, I
	   believe that the "slow to a crawl" implementation is superior
	   to the current "eat till you choke".

	d) While I favor the removal of the "Stop eating?" prompt, at the
	   same time I also support meal-interruption at the point of 
	   attack.

